the European Union has announced  sanctions on Iran's Security Forces over  the death of the 22-year-old woman Gina  Masa amini last month and the repression  of subsequent anti-government protests  EU foreign ministers agreed to Target  Iran's so-called morality police and  whose hands amini died as well as a  cabinet minister overseeing internet  blockages the named officials will be  subject to EU Visa bans and asset  freezes  support for the protesters and  condemnation of the brutal Crackdown EU  diplomats gathered in Luxembourg to send  a strong message to Iran's leadership  today we are making a clear that we are  taking those responsible who use brutal  force against citizens against women  against men against teenagers just  because they want to live their life as  we do and freedom and peace  for more than a month now Iran's leaders  have struggled to contain protests  sparked by the death of Gina Maxa amini  after she was arrested by the so-called  morality police the protest quickly  morphed into anti-government  demonstrations activists say some 200  people have been killed  announcing the new sanctions they use  foreign policy chief called on Iran to  end the violence adding that if  necessary the block will go further  the council adopted today restrictive  measures against 11 individuals  on four entities  targeting those linked to the death of  my shamini  and to the repression of peaceful  protesters  and if needed  we are ready to add more names to this  list  Iran has vowed an immediate response to  the new sanctions  such rushed and biased behavior is  unacceptable it's meddling in our  domestic affairs we don't accept this  as politicians trade accusations  protesters like these students in  central Iran continue venting their  anger at the authorities who seem  unwilling to listen  and for more we can speak to gisio Nia  she's the director of the Atlantic  council's strategic litigation project  focused on accountability for human  rights violations her area of expertise  is rule of law in Iran Miss Mia what do  you make of these new EU sanctions what  effect if any do you think they'll have  well it's certainly a welcome  development these sanctions on 15  Iranian entities and individuals who are  implicated in human rights violations  including Iran's morality police and its  law enforcement forces it's very  appreciated because this is the only  second such set of Human Rights  designations  um that the EU has levied against the  Islamic Republic since 2013.  so human rights lawyers and Advocates  have long been calling for further  designations for human rights violations  on top Iranian leadership and that just  hadn't happened until now  are there other levers you would like  the International Community to pull to  hold the Iranian government accountable  yes there's a lot that the International  Community can be doing right now and  that the EU and its individual member  states can be doing  um one is that member states need to  immediately call a special session of  the UN Human Rights Council there needs  to be a special session to discuss the  ongoing human rights crisis in Iran and  to discuss the setup of an  accountability mechanism that will allow  for a repository to streamline the  collection and preservation of evidence  of violations and to possibly issue  reports on the situation so that is  something that the International  Community and U.N member states can do  today if they wanted to an additional  thing that they could do is remove the  Islamic Republic Iran of Iran from the  UN Commission on the status of women  which is the un's top women's rights  body they were elected last year year  and they started a four-year term in  March 2022 they're going to be there  until 2026.  um a country which has gender  discriminatory laws on the books to the  severity that the Islamic Republic does  should not be allowed to have a leader  leadership position on a U.N body like  that  the traditional thing that can happen  yeah sorry sorry continue  I was going to say additional thing that  could happen with respect to these  sanctions so we saw that there are these  human rights sanctions that have been  issued  um  it's fine to freeze the assets of the  individuals who are implicated in these  violations and who are responsible for  them but I also think that EU countries  should start considering legislation  that allows for the seizure of these  assets of chronic human rights violators  and transfer from perpetrators to  victims similar to what Canada has done  they recently passed legislation that  allows for that repurposing of assets  yeah these protests have been going on  for over a month now and despite the  deadly Crackdown they don't appear to be  abating I want to look ahead with you  what is your sense of how this is going  to play out  the Islamic Republic of Iran has  successfully squelched out previous  protests that have been reoccurring in  the country since December 2017. I think  what is different here is that young  girls young women have joined these  protests it this is about social change  they have joined by taking off their  mandatory hijab and peacefully  protesting for their rights I think it  becomes much more difficult for State  security forces to kill young girls  girls as young as 12 who are protesting  for their rights so that's why we see  these protests enduring and really  because the core is it's about social  change they are demanding social  freedoms yeah specifically with regard  to the fire in the Evan prison where  many protesters are being held what are  you hearing about what's happening there  and how concerned are you about the  welfare of the prisoners  well we've been hearing different things  Iranian State media and the Judiciary  first reported that 40 people were  killed in the fire then they quickly  revised that number to four now they're  saying that eight people have died  because an additional Force succumbed to  their injuries so we don't have a  transparent death count human rights  organizations and citizen journalist  collectives are trying to establish the  welfare of different individuals but we  should remember that there are lots of  political prisoners and Evin there are  human rights lawyers there are those who  are in prison just for their peaceful  expression of their freedom of  expression Association and assembly and  just for doing their jobs so  um this is very critical the prison is  overcrowded right now because the  authorities have rounded up a lot of  different Civil Society leaders there  and this really demands the  international community's attention and  anybody you know and any countries that  have diplomatic relations with the  Islamic Republic should be urging the  absolute welfare and release of these  prisoners okay Sonia with the Atlantic  Council thank you so much  thank you
 

